features/sea-compact/decisions.md-9-- Corrected after design challenge attempt 1. The tile's 256 sub-units, the 64 headings and `turnRate` 1 stay unchanged: 1 is already the minimum, and the claim tests depend on 256. So full speed is 39 sub-units per step (3.05 tiles/s), and handling is judged on screen: time to full speed, stopping distance, 3.2 s per full turn, and the turning circle in pixels.
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-10-- "Ships meet" becomes a check, not a hope: every neighbour route passes within sight of one central `hub` point, because shoals block the direct routes along the edges. At about 3 tiles/s, the detour through the centre also keeps neighbours inside §3's 20–40 s.
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-11-- "Reduce the scale" also means drawing larger. Tiles go from 16 to about 24 px, so the 10-tile sight circle fits on the 800 × 560 view and on-screen speed stays about the same (80 → 72 px/s). The sight radius itself stays 10 tiles.
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-12-- Density is a validator, not a judgement: no navigable tile may be farther than the sight radius from a non-sea tile, and routes are funnelled through channels. This follows §3 ("Remove empty stretches before adding more sailing mechanics"), so there are no new mechanics (slow zones, currents).
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-13-- Still four small islands (§18 stage 2). A `rock` tile type for outcrops and landmarks is optional; shoals alone are acceptable.
features/sea-compact/decisions.md:14:- The flaky `sail-and-dock` browser test (seen in duel-online-fixes-001's worker phase) is fixed here, since this feature rewrites its route anyway: the test brakes until the dock hint reads "Press E to dock", then taps E, and it must pass 5 repeats.
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-15-- One fullstack lane (`world`) with all five checks and all six browser scenario ids. The owned paths list the sea files one by one, so the assistant's parallel ledger and claim fixes (`packages/*/src/objectives`, `packages/*/src/claim`) stay disjoint.
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-16-- Every acceptance item names its test, because most earlier blocked reviews came from acceptance items without one.
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-17-
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-18-## Assumptions
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-19-
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-20-- The operator confirmed the objectives-ledger defaults: the repeat-win gold window counts from any previous win, paid or not; a winner who leaves during the 5 s theft choice takes nothing.
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-21-- The encounters slice will run the duel inside `MatchRoom` as a simulation object, as §14 requires ("Duels are isolated simulation objects within that room"), reusing the `simulation/netcode` core. The standalone `DuelRoom` stays as the practice arena. This is recorded for `encounters-theft` and doesn't change this feature.
features/sea-compact/decisions.md-22-- The protocol version stays: the message shapes don't change, and the web and server always ship together.
--
features/sea-compact/world-task.md-31-   - *Density:* for every navigable tile, the integer squared Euclidean distance (tile centre to tile centre) to the nearest land, shoal, dock or rock tile is at most `SIGHT_RADIUS_TILES`². The map edge doesn't count as an obstacle.
features/sea-compact/world-task.md-32-   - *A shared crossing:* the map has one `hub` point (a Tiled point object in open sea near the centre). Every shortest sea route between neighbouring islands, dock to dock, passes within `SIGHT_RADIUS_TILES` of it, so ships crossing between any two islands can see each other there. Shoal bars and outcrops block the direct routes along the map's edges and funnel traffic through the hub's channels.
features/sea-compact/world-task.md-33-
features/sea-compact/world-task.md-34-   `npm run map:build` prints the largest obstacle distance, the open-sea share and each route's closest approach to the hub.
features/sea-compact/world-task.md-35-4. **Rock outcrops (optional).** If you add a `rock` tile type (impassable, not an island, no dock, drawn as a landmark), it is part of the documented Tiled subset (`tiledMap.ts`, `assets/source/maps/README.md`), the collision rules and the minimap, with unit tests for each. Shoals alone are also acceptable.
features/sea-compact/world-task.md-36-5. **Spawn fairness kept.** There are eight spawn slots on the outer sea, adjacent slots out of engagement range, every slot reachable by sea. The existing validators prove this.
features/sea-compact/world-task.md-37-6. **Larger scale on screen.** Tiles are drawn at about 24 px (today 16), so the whole sight circle fits inside the 800 × 560 view. On-screen ship speed stays close to today's (about 72–80 px/s). A web unit test asserts that the sight circle's diameter in pixels fits within the view height, and the camera still follows the ship and stays inside the map. Terrain, fog and ship pixel tests keep passing at the new scale.
features/sea-compact/world-task.md-38-7. **Visibility unchanged.** The integration visibility tests pass on the new map, using positions taken from the map.
features/sea-compact/world-task.md:39:8. **`sail-and-dock` is stable.** In the worker phase it once failed under load: the ship coasted past the dock reach with E held. The test now approaches like a careful player. It stops a few tiles off the dock, creeps in with short throttle taps, and releases every key as soon as the HUD's dock hint reads "Press E to dock at …". Then it taps E. It never holds brake at a stop, which reverses the ship. It must pass 5 runs in a row locally (`--repeat-each=5` on that spec). Report the count.
features/sea-compact/world-task.md-40-9. **Coordinates and limits come from the map.** `testPlacement`'s tile bounds and every test position are derived from the map or content constants, not the old map's numbers. In `out-of-sight`, the two ships are 12–13 tiles apart on a row where the camera isn't clamped, so the unseen ship would be on the canvas if it were drawn. A test asserts that its position is inside the view.
features/sea-compact/world-task.md-41-10. **Docs.** `docs/architecture.md` (the map, sailing numbers, drawing scale) and `assets/source/maps/README.md` describe the new map and any new tile type, within today's length.
